From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 09:14:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: replay user mappings concurrently

Threads of one process replayed their page-table changes to the Darwin
software MMU one at a time, behind a sleeping per-mm turnstile taken with
mmap_read_lock() held. A walk holds it for three or four microseconds and
a waiter that sleeps pays a wakeup of several hundred, so eight threads
of a 4 KiB guest that fault on fresh memory spent more time queued than
running, and their vCPUs idled meanwhile: in one multi-core Geekbench
Navigation run they waited 28 seconds in total on the turnstile for 2.6 of
walking. A 4 KiB guest takes four times the faults of a 16 KiB one and
bounded every x86-64 guest with it.

Four changes, which a 4 KiB guest needs together:

- A fault replayed the faulting page before asking Linux to resolve it,
  in case the page was only missing from the shadow MMU. That is what a
  fork or an mprotect() leaves behind, but seven in 388733 demand-paging
  faults were of that kind. um_tlb_access_granted() samples the PTE
  without a lock, and only a PTE which already grants the access is
  replayed first. The answer is a hint: a stale one costs a spurious
  fault or the replay it would have skipped.

- Walkers of one user mm run together. Nothing in Linux orders them; the
  host does. batch_begin() records the generation's invalidation epoch,
  Linux invalidates the host range after it changes a PTE and before it
  frees the page, every such invalidation advances the epoch, and
  batch_end() publishes only while it still matches. What two walkers of
  one page can still do is publish in the wrong order across a change
  that needs no invalidation, and each of those makes the PTE more
  permissive, so the stale batch leaves the page absent or less
  accessible than Linux allows and the next access replays it. The
  caller's own range joins its snapshot under one lock hold, so a sibling
  cannot take it and leave the caller to return to an unpublished page.
  The host walker call remains as the carrier's binding to the
  generation; a host which still treats it as exclusive serializes the
  walkers again, which is slower and no less correct.

- A replay is published in batches of 64 pages, which span the ranges of
  its snapshot, and only a rejected batch is walked again. After a fork, an exec or a flush_tlb_mm() the
  pending range is the whole mm, and every sibling invalidation rejects
  whatever batch is open: one batch for all of it started over for as
  long as a sibling kept unmapping, with no scheduling point on the way.
  An Android boot held a CPU in current_mm_sync() for 84 seconds, RCU
  reported the stall and the boot took 621 seconds instead of 182. A
  batch which was published stays sound when a later one is rejected,
  because the invalidation that rejects it has already removed what it
  covers from the host. Between batches no page-table lock, host batch
  or walker binding is live, so after every batch it fills and every
  sixteenth it loses the walker services its clock event and its IPIs
  there and lets the scheduler run. A batch per range was tried first:
  a fault's snapshot is a handful of single pages, every batch_end()
  takes the generation's mappings lock exclusively, and eight threads
  touching fresh memory lost a tenth of their throughput to it.

- A user mm keeps exact pending ranges. They were rounded to the 16 KiB
  Darwin host page, which is what a kernel mapping is made of, but a user
  mm runs through the software MMU one Linux page at a time, and every
  page a 4 KiB guest touched walked and published its three neighbours
  again. The replay in front of a granted fault still takes the whole
  16 KiB, because there the neighbours are usually missing as well.

The Darwin enter_turnstile() and exit_turnstile() have no callers left
and go; init_mm keeps its own serialized walker.

Eight vCPUs, 4 KiB pages, same-session controls, with the host changes
this needs: first-touch of fresh anonymous memory 593 -> 1490 MiB/s on
one thread and 325 -> 995 on eight; multi-core File Compression 584 ->
800, Navigation 997 -> 1444, Background Blur 721 -> 770, HDR 842 -> 896.
